  "answer": "The bug is clear: `total` is annotated as `string` while `reduce` produces a number — a type inconsistency, plus an unnecessary `Number(...)` round-trip. Fixing the annotation:\nNow verifying both type-consistency and the test:\nDone. Summary:\n\n**Root cause:** In `src/calc.ts`, the running total was annotated `const total: string` even though `values.reduce((sum, value) => sum + value, 0)` produces a `number` — the declared type didn't match the actual value, and the code papered over it with a runtime `Number(total)` coercion.\n\n**Fix (signature unchanged: `average(values: number[]): number`):**\n- Changed the annotation to `const total: number`, so the running total is genuinely a number.\n- Removed the now-redundant `Number(total)` cast — the return is simply `total / values.length`.\n\n**Verification:**\n- `tsc --noEmit --strict src/calc.ts` → clean, exit 0 (type-consistent).\n- `bun test` → **1 pass, 0 fail** (`average([2, 4]) === 3`, `average([]) === 0`).\n\nNo other files were touched; AGENTS.md, package.json, and test/oracle.test.ts are preserved."
